  "slug": "edna-diversity-monkfish-759e",
  "summary": "Long-read and low-input methods now recover species identities and genomes from century-old and single-larva material.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is molecular inference of species identity, diversity and genomic features from limited or archival material. Nanopore sequencing underpins a barcoding survey of 3,657 European Ulva samples from 30 countries, identifying 46 species and flagging non-indigenous taxa, and a mini-barcoding workflow that recovered COI sequences from 284 of 288 century-old spider museum specimens, adding 53 new BINs to BOLD. Multiple displacement amplification enables WGS and GBS genotyping of single zebra mussel veliger larvae from about 1 ng DNA. Off-target sequencing of 312 spruce bark beetles reveals frequent nematode associates across Europe, and a Nanopore-based annotated reference genome is reported for the Critically Endangered Bermuda skink. What is new is the application of these approaches to low-input and century-old material.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
